Title: "Labels"
Id: "62a24a65-05c9-4e09-9722-1eb355fe2ce5"

Text: "Labels identify items inside brackets or nested groups.

```mlg-fragment
[\theorem.ordinal.trichotomy]
Theorem:
then: ...
```

Label headers are parsed from dotted parts. Dots are commonly rendered as spaces in fallback display names, with words capitalized for theorem-like and axiom-like cards.

Mathlingua distinguishes four kinds of labels:

1. Structural Label Headers: Square-bracketed headings before a top-level group or clause (such as `[theorem.name]`, or `[step]` preceding an entry in `Justification:`).

2. Line-Level Labels: Attached to single-line formulations or text items using `. [label]: ...`:

```mlg-fragment
then:
. [base_case]: x > 0
. [note]: \"Check inductive step next\"
```

Line labels must appear on their own indented line; inline arguments (such as `forAll: [l]: x > 0`) cannot have labels.

3. Expression Justification Labels: Attached to grouped formulations using `[:label:]` or multi-labels `[:l1, l2:]`:

```mlg-fragment
(x + y)[:sum:]
(.x is \real.)[:typed, positive:]
```

A `[:label:]` is discharged by a matching `[label]` entry in the enclosing item's `Justification:` section.

4. Reference Citation Labels: Attached to grouped formulations using `(:label:)` or in prose using `&#39;&#39;text&#39;&#39;(&#58;label&#58;)`:

```mlg-fragment
(x = x)(:ref_label:)
(x = x)[:just_label:](:ref_label:)
```

Reference labels must match a labeled entry `. [label]: $resource` in the enclosing item's `References:` section.

Unlike structural label headers, expression labels and reference labels are lexer tokens and do not support stropped symbolic parts."
Id: "d36979a8-c1ec-4f3b-8e8c-3dafd5ee366b"
